[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m The volume \(V\) of a cylinder is given by the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[ V_{\text{cylinder}} = \pi r^2 h \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m where \(r\) is the radius and \(h\) is the height of the cylinder.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m The volume \(V\) of a sphere is given by the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[ V_{\text{sphere}} = \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m where \(r\) is the radius of the sphere.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Given:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m - The radius of the cylindrical beaker (\(r_{\text{cylinder}}\)) is 3 cm.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m - The height of the cylindrical beaker (\(h_{\text{cylinder}}\)) is 8 cm.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m - The radius of the spherical tank (\(r_{\text{sphere}}\)) is 6 cm.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m First, let's calculate the volume of the cylindrical beaker:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[ V_{\text{cylinder}} = \pi (3)^2 (8) = 72\pi \, \text{cm}^3 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Next, let's calculate the volume of the spherical tank:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[ V_{\text{sphere}} = \frac{4}{3} \pi (6)^3 = \frac{4}{3} \pi (216) = 288\pi \, \text{cm}^3 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Now, to find out how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill the spherical tank, we divide the volume of the spherical tank by the volume of one cylindrical beaker:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[ \text{Number of beakers} = \frac{V_{\text{sphere}}}{V_{\text{cylinder}}} = \frac{288\pi}{72\pi} = \frac{288}{72} = 4 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Therefore, it will take \(\boxed{4}\) cylindrical beakers to fill the spherical tank.
