[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m [response] First, we need to calculate the volume of the cylindrical beaker and the volume of the spherical tank.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m The formula for the volume of a cylinder is \(V_{cylinder} = \pi r^2 h\), where \(r\) is the radius and \(h\) is the height.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m The formula for the volume of a sphere is \(V_{sphere} = \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3\), where \(r\) is the radius.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Given that the cylindrical beaker has a radius of 3 cm and a height of 8 cm, we can calculate the volume of the beaker as follows:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[V_{cylinder} = \pi \times 3^2 \times 8 = 72\pi \, \text{cm}^3\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Given that the spherical tank has a radius of 6 cm, we can calculate the volume of the tank as follows:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[V_{sphere} = \frac{4}{3} \pi \times 6^3 = \frac{4}{3} \pi \times 216 = 288\pi \, \text{cm}^3\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m To find out how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill the spherical tank, we divide the volume of the spherical tank by the volume of the cylindrical beaker:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[\frac{V_{sphere}}{V_{cylinder}} = \frac{288\pi}{72\pi} = 4\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Therefore, it will take \boxed{4} cylindrical beakers to fill the spherical tank.
