[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m [response] To find the speed of the particle, we need to find the magnitude of the velocity vector. The position of the particle at time $t$ is given by $\left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4}, 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)$. 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m The velocity vector is the derivative of the position vector with respect to time $t$. 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Let's compute the derivatives of the components of the position vector:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m For the $x$-component: 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[ \frac{d}{dt} \left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4} \right) = 3 \cdot \frac{1}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4} = \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4} \]
