[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m First, note that a regular octahedron can be visualized as two pyramids with a square base joined at their bases. The vertices of the octahedron can be paired such that the distance between any two opposite vertices is the same.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Let's denote the vertices of the octahedron as \(A, B, C, D, E, F\) such that \(A, B\) are opposite, \(C, D\) are opposite, and \(E, F\) are opposite. Without loss of generality, assume the distances given are to vertices \(A, B, C, D, E\). The distance to the sixth vertex \(F\) can be found using the property of the regular octahedron that the sum of the squares of the distances from any point to opposite vertices is constant.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m Let the distance from \(P\) to \(F\) be \(x\). Then we have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=88572)[0m \[3^2 + 7^2 + 8^2 + 9^2 + 11^2 + x^2 = \text{constant}\]
